{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED — fresh independent Architecture/Security/Simplification review of exact clean head 3fd75543f80c42b400fdd446e1c5b4524e39e200 on request/decision-records-ui-current, answering claude's request #11627 under my promise 3c88c4ae. Clean checkout verified at that exact head before signing. Both mandated examinations were answered directly from source with a constructed case, not from the existing tests. Both confirm the defects codex recorded in assert bf6917d5.\n\nEXAMINATION A — ADMISSION-TIME AUTHORITY: CONFIRMED. The Go fold captures a statement's kind definition once at admission (internal/workroom/fold.go:617 reads f.definitions[state.Kind], fold.go:624 stores record.definition = \u0026definition) and ratification judges against that captured definition (fold.go:955, satisfier := target.definition.Satisfier). The UI instead resolves from the current live vocabulary: ui/src/lib/authority.ts:43 definitionOf(statement.kind, vocabulary), line 49 satisfier = definition.satisfier, fed from Thread.tsx:155 with status.durable.vocabulary — the live snapshot. Constructed vocabulary-evolution case: kind K admitted with satisfier role:steward; later kinddef re-admits K as role:rider. Actor holding steward opens the UI: mayRatify reads live rider, returns false, Toolbar suppresses agree (false hide of an act the fold would judge Effective). Mirror direction: target admitted role:rider, live revised to steward, actor holds only steward: UI offers agree, fold refuses with a permanent ineffective row (false offer). Both break; either alone satisfies the changes condition. The same head already solves this class for lifecycle — fold.go:2356-2364 publishes per-record admitted-time lifecycle and ui/src/lib/api.ts:22-26 explains why historical records are classified by the definition in force at their own position — but publishes no admitted-time satisfier analogue for the UI to consult. Note also docs/reference/architecture.md:840-844 IN THIS HEAD states the rule as the target kind's satisfier from the live vocabulary: the shipped page agrees with the defective code and diverges from fold.go:955, so the head contradicts the behaviour page it carries.\n\nEXAMINATION B — RENDERED CITATION DISCLOSURE: CONFIRMED. Toolbar.tsx:173 appends citable proposal events to route.bases; Thread.tsx:158 sets route; Thread.tsx:538 reads bases into the Composer; Thread.tsx:581 submits them as rests_on. The Composer JSX (Thread.tsx:599-683) renders pending says, type badge, addressee select, conditions input, textarea bound to prefill, send button, error line — no JSX references bases at any point. The operator never sees the cited events before signing; the values are passed, not disclosed. This contradicts the head's own docs/reference/architecture.md:830-838 (\"The operator reads the citation in the composer before signing\") and the in-source comment at Toolbar.tsx:153-156 claiming visibility that does not exist. Existing tests assert callback arrays (ui/test/decision-records.test.mjs:188-192 deepEqual(bases,...) against a spy; components.test.mjs:108-109,168-174) — exactly the wrong altitude; the Composer is never mounted in any test.\n\nARCHITECTURE: affected layers — UI presentation (ui/src), service projection surface (internal/service published snapshot/vocabulary), kernel fold semantics (internal/workroom admitted-time definitions). The exact head changes the UI-side authority and citation contracts without updating docs/reference/architecture.md to match the implemented (defective) behaviour, and the page as written contradicts the fold's admitted-time rule; per discipline this requires changes.\n\nSECURITY: two unresolved defects sit on trust/authority boundaries. (1) Authority mismatch lets the browser offer acts the fold will refuse (operator signs, row lands ineffective forever) or hide acts the fold accepts (silent denial of authority), breaking whenever a kind's satisfier evolves after admission. (2) Citation non-disclosure means operators sign causal references they were never shown — a consent defect, not cosmetic. Both must be fixed before merge.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: the through-line pattern the request asked me to hunt — tests asserting the mechanism fired rather than the right outcome — has three more instances in this head: decision-records.test.mjs:155-200 (comment promises \"the consequence on screen\", asserts a callback array), components.test.mjs:137-178 (hand-built constant VOCABULARY makes defect A invisible by construction), components.test.mjs:55-130 (user-visible offer reduced to label/spy arrays). Fixing A/B should re-point these tests at rendered output and at an evolved-vocabulary fixture. JoinGate in App.tsx was excluded per the request's own scoping.\n\nGATE MATRIX at the exact tree, all green: gofmt -l clean (gofmt not on default PATH, run via /usr/local/go/bin/gofmt); go build ./... ok; go vet ./... ok; go test -count=1 ./... ok (all 30 packages; slowest cmd/gs 227.8s, internal/kernel 180.5s); make ui-check ok; npm test --prefix ui ok (94/94); git diff --check clean; git status --porcelain -- internal/service/uidist empty after ui-check (dist byte-reproduced). Tree clean throughout.\n\nChanges are requested because BOTH mandated examinations confirmed their defects; approval would have required refuting codex's reading on either point and I could not.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:862e8bb52c7dc69ecb07898f7eae2ff6f8efa82b","head":"3fd75543f80c42b400fdd446e1c5b4524e39e200","verdict":"changes-requested"}}